---
/* THE NICOTRA — the maternal Sicilian line, as arena.astro is for the Arena.
 *
 * Three days ago this line was two names on a Queensland death registration
 * and nothing else: «John Nicotra» and «Venera Cassaneti», parents of Maria,
 * with no dates, no places and no act. It now runs to generation six on both
 * sides and crosses four comuni — and the man it is named for is still lost.
 *
 * The page is built to hold BOTH halves of that: what four acts established,
 * and what twenty-seven read years have failed to.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import additions from "../data/additions.json";
import nic from "../data/nicotra.json";

const adds = additions.filter((a) => /Nicotra|Cassaniti|Murabito|Currenti/.test(a.name));


const LINE = nic.line;
const total = nic.read;
---
<Base title="The Nicotra" eyebrow="Mascali · Giarre · Riposto · Piedimonte Etneo"
      dek="Three days ago this line was two names on a Queensland death certificate. It now reaches generation six on both sides, crosses four comuni — and the man it is named for is still missing."

>
  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Where it started</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Everything this archive knew about the Nicotra came from one Australian document: the
      <strong>1963 Queensland death registration</strong> of <P n="Maria Nicotra" />, which names her
      parents as <strong>«John Nicotra»</strong> and <strong>«Venera Cassaneti»</strong>. No dates, no
      places, no act — and one of those surnames <strong>does not exist in Italy</strong>. «Cassaneti»
      appears exactly once in the whole Queensland index, on that certificate, taken down from an
      informant at a Brisbane registry counter. The Sicilian form is
      <strong>CASSANITI</strong>, and a register searched for the other spelling returns nothing
      forever.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The line, as four acts give it</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">Read in the original, September 2026. Not one of these
    people is in the family's export with a date.</p>
    <div class="prose">
      {LINE.map((r) => (
        <div style="margin-top:1.3rem;padding-left:1rem;border-left:3px solid var(--rule)">
          <div class="lab">generation {r.gen}</div>
          <h3 style="margin:.3rem 0 .4rem">{r.who}</h3>
          <p style="margin:0">{r.what}</p>
          <p class="small" style="margin-top:.4rem;color:var(--ink-3)">{r.src}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:20px"><strong>The join is not circular, which matters because
    «Giovanni Nicotra» is a common name.</strong> The family's own export — parsed long before any of
    this — gives Giovanni and Venera <strong>seven children, and one of them is a ROSARIO</strong>. A
    first son takes the paternal grandfather's name, and the paternal grandfather in the 1872 act is
    Rosario. This archive held the grandson's name for months and could not have derived the
    grandfather's from it.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What {total} read years have not found</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">Rosario Nicotra and Venera Murabito are named in one
    act and appear in no register.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Comune</th><th>Series</th><th class="m">Years read</th><th>What it said</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {Object.entries(nic.byComune).map(([c, rows]) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{c}</strong></td>
              <td class="small">Nati {rows[0].year}–{rows[rows.length - 1].year}</td>
              <td class="m">{rows.filter((r) => r.entries !== "\u2014").length}</td>
              <td class="small">{rows.filter((r) => !["\u2014", "none", "empty"].includes(r.entries)).length
                ? `${rows.filter((r) => !["\u2014", "none", "empty"].includes(r.entries)).length} year(s) hold an N entry; none of the fathers is a Rosario`
                : "not one N entry in any year read"}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>The instrument is not the problem.</strong> Every
    one of these Tavole is alphabetical and names both parents, so the household would announce itself.
    What is being tested is <strong>whether the man's own statements about himself are true</strong> —
    he is <em>trentuno</em> in 1872 and <em>ventotto</em> in 1875, six years apart in three — and
    whether «nato a Giarre» means the comune or the district.</p>
  </section>

  {adds.length ? (
  <section class="blk">
    <h2>The people these acts added</h2>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:12px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th class="m">Gen</th><th class="m">Place</th><th>Trade</th><th class="m">In the tree</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {adds.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td><strong>{a.name}</strong></td>
              <td class="m">{a.gen}</td>
              <td class="m small">{a.place}</td>
              <td class="small">{a.trade}</td>
              <td class="m small">{a.inTree && a.inTree !== "—" ? "yes" : <em>no</em>}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>) : null}

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>What would carry it further</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p><strong>One row in a marriage <em>Tavola</em>.</strong> The restauration marriage index of
      any of these comuni names <strong>both spouses and all four of their parents</strong>, and marks
      the dead with <em>«fu»</em>. <strong>Rosario Nicotra × Venera Murabito's own marriage — about
      1840 — would hand over generation seven in a single line</strong>, and would say which town each
      of them came from, which is the question the whole search now turns on.</p>
      <p><a href={u("/riposto/")}>Riposto, where the search stands →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/giarre/")}>Giarre, and its negative →</a> ·
      <a href={u("/mascali/")}>Mascali, and her birth act →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
